WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.300
Template-heavy code needs a different testing mindset.

2
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.880
It is not enough to test one happy-path instantiation.

3
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:23.260
Generic code should be exercised across common types, boundary types, move-only types, non-copyable types, throwing operations where relevant, custom comparators, custom allocators, views, iterators, and policy objects.

4
00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.700
Invalid use should fail clearly.

5
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:32.520
When the build system supports negative compile checks, tests can verify that unintended instantiations are rejected.

6
00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:37.260
This protects the contract from gradually becoming broader than the design intended.

7
00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.840
Clear compile-time diagnostics are part of usability and safety.

8
00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:49.240
Compiler warnings, sanitizers, static analysis, and strict diagnostic builds can expose issues earlier.

9
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:58.620
They can reveal lifetime mistakes, incorrect assumptions about moves or copies, suspicious conversions, and runtime behavior in representative instantiations.

10
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.000
Template libraries often benefit from more than one compiler configuration because diagnostics differ.

11
00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:16.440
Review should check constraints, lifetime assumptions, overload behavior, customization points, specialization paths, diagnostics, and whether invalid use fails in a helpful way.

12
00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:22.780
Reviewers should ask which type families were tested and which type-dependent behaviors are expected.

13
00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:29.060
When a template defect is found, capture the specific type or instantiation pattern that revealed it.

14
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.600
That regression test becomes part of the generic contract.

15
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.800
Diagnostics should be treated as part of the developer experience.

16
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:43.860
If safe use requires reading pages of compiler output, callers may work around the template instead of using it correctly.
